---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getSnapshot } from '../lib/data';
import { formatDate, formatNumber } from '../lib/format';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

const snapshot = getSnapshot();
const other = snapshot.domains.find((domain) => domain.id === 'other');
---

<Base title="About the data" description="Where the data comes from, how often it is refreshed, and how domains, momentum and similarity are worked out.">
  <article class="prose">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1>About the data</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {`This site lists every public GitHub repository with at least ${formatNumber(snapshot.minimum_stars)} stars: ${formatNumber(snapshot.repository_count)} of them as of ${formatDate(snapshot.generated_at)}.`}
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
    <p>
      Everything is read from GitHub's public search API. GitHub returns at most 1,000 results per search, so the
      fetch splits the star range into slices that each stay under that limit and stitches them together. A
      scheduled job does this once a day, rebuilds the site and publishes it. Nothing is scraped, and the site
      has no server: it is static files plus the data you see.
    </p>

    <h2>What a rank means</h2>
    <p>
      Rank is position by star count at snapshot time, nothing more. Stars measure attention, not quality,
      maintenance or fitness for your use. Ages and "last push" figures are measured from the snapshot time,
      not from when you open the page.
    </p>

    <h2>Momentum</h2>
    <p>
      Each daily run stores every repository's star count. Gains per day, 7 days and 30 days compare today with
      the nearest stored day at least that far back. If snapshots are further apart than the period, the gain is
      scaled to the period, and if the nearest one is more than two weeks too old the figure is left out rather
      than guessed. A repository is "new to the list" for two weeks after it first appears.
      {
        snapshot.history.days < 2
          ? ' Only one snapshot exists so far, so these figures start with the next refresh.'
          : ` ${snapshot.history.days} snapshots are currently in use.`
      }
    </p>

    <h2>Weekly reports</h2>
    <p>
      Each <a href={href('/weekly/')}>weekly report</a> compares the last snapshot of a week with the last one
      before that week began, and says which two dates those were. A finished week's report is stored and never
      recomputed, so its page stays the same. The current week's report is provisional until the week ends.
    </p>

    <h2>Maintenance figures</h2>
    <p>
      Latest release and open issue and pull request counts come from GitHub at snapshot time. They are shown
      as plain facts, not rolled into a score: a project with no releases may simply not use them, and a large
      issue count often means many users, not neglect.
    </p>

    <h2>Domains</h2>
    <p>
      Domains are assigned from an editorial taxonomy, not by GitHub. A repository scores points for matching
      topics, for whole-word matches in its name and description, and a little for its language. Language alone
      is never enough: a Python web framework is not filed under machine learning just for being Python. A
      repository with no clear signal lands in "Other" ({formatNumber(other?.count ?? 0)} today) instead of being
      forced into a category.
    </p>

    <h2>Similar repositories</h2>
    <p>
      Similarity is topic overlap, weighted so that rare topics count for much more than common ones. Two
      projects that both use <code>raft</code> are more alike than two that both use <code>python</code>.
      Repositories without topics have no similar list.
    </p>

    <h2>"Understand this repo" links</h2>
    <p>
      Every repository links to four independent sites that explain it, diagram it, turn it into a text digest
      for an AI assistant, or write a prompt that would rebuild it. They are third-party services that work by
      swapping <code>github.com</code> in the address for their own domain. This site only links to them and
      has no say over their availability or output.
    </p>

    <h2>Your lists, comparisons and stars</h2>
    <p>
      Saved lists and the comparison selection live in your browser's local storage. They are never uploaded.
      A share link carries the list inside the link itself, so whoever opens it gets their own copy.
      <a href={href('/stars/')}>Check my stars</a> reads a username's public stars straight from GitHub in
      your browser; nothing passes through this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Reuse</h2>
    <p>
      The full dataset behind this site is available as <a href={href('/data/snapshot.json')}>snapshot.json</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
